as part of their rectangle.\n2. For each rectangle, count the cells that are not the dominant colour 1.\n3. Crop and output the rectangle with the smallest count.\n4. If two rectangles tie, prefer the lower ratio of special cells to area, then the larger rectangle. This tie-break is my own guess, not something the training pairs show.\n\n**Applying it to the test.** The program applies the same procedure to the 22×25 test grid. I have not counted the test rectangles by hand. If two of them tie on the fewest specials, the guessed tie-break decides which is output.\n\n**Teaching stages.** The first `emit` shows the chosen rectangle highlighted on the input canvas: its 1-cells are temporarily recoloured with a colour that does not otherwise appear. The second `emit` shows the final crop.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    comps = components(g != bg)\n    best = None\n    for cells in comps:\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(cells)\n        sub = g[y0:y1+1, x0:x1+1]\n        vals, cnts = np.unique(sub[sub != bg], return_counts=True)\n        main = vals[np.argmax(cnts)]\n        spec = int(np.sum((sub != bg) & (sub != main)))\n        area = len(cells)\n        key = (spec, spec / area, -area)\n        if best is None or key < best[0]:\n            best = (key, (y0, x0, y1, x1), main)\n    y0, x0, y1, x1 = best[1]\n    main = best[2]\n    hl = g.copy()\n    used = set(np.unique(g).tolist())\n    mark = next(c for c in range(10) if c not in used)\n    region = hl[y0:y1+1, x0:x1+1]\n    region[region == main] = mark\n    emit(hl, \"Highlight the rectangle with the fewest special (non-main-colour) cells.\")\n    out = crop(g, y0, x0, y1, x1)\n    emit(out, \"Crop that rectangle as the output.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Connected components of this mask give the rectangles, with the 4 and 6 cells included as part of their rectangle."}],"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The chosen rectangle is always the one with the **fewest special cells**."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Crop and output the rectangle with the smallest count."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"If two of them tie on the fewest specials, the guessed tie-break decides which is output."}],"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"irst `emit` shows the chosen rectangle highlighted on the input canvas: its 1-cells are temporarily recoloured with a colour that does not otherwise appear."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"These are hand counts."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"For each rectangle, count the cells that are not the dominant colour 1."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Crop and output the rectangle with the smallest count."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"In every pair the output is an exact crop of one of these rectangles, so the task is to work out which rectangle gets chosen."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Crop and output the rectangle with the smallest count."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The second `emit` shows the final crop."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"3d625f638ac7074bc8c76566cb60b18cec6c8efeb58497ad1d716d36998daf21","task_sha256":"fbf3688c0b09af7993076b14d9327ae0ade41b049e02e94fbb5f8daf5bb8f773","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
